sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 native Swamp schedules, one Swamp workflow per Schedule (Nick 2026-11-02), nothing installed on Nodes; R1-R3.
  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.
  %% status: migrated
  %% command: schedule:destroy
  %% kind: schedule.destroy
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: schedule:destroy 7f3c…<br/>Schedule UUID
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit schedule:destroy 7f3c9a2e-41d0-4b8a-9c11-2f6e0d5b7a10`
  %%> - syntax: `<schedule> [--project=ID] [--yes] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the UUID of the Schedule
  %%> Other form:
  %%> - `<name> --project=ID` deletes a Project Schedule definition; see below
  %%> Optional:
  %%> - `--yes` skips the prompt; `--idempotency-key` and `--json` change no step
  %%> Confirmation:
  %%> - without `--yes`, the CLI asks first; the default is No
  %%> - without a terminal, or with `--json`, `--yes` is required
  %%> With `--project`:
  %%> - only the Project Schedule definition is deleted (R1); Instance copies stay
  Note over GW: Validate, mark removing<br/>Schedule 7f3c… · Instance 42
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42
  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… exists; a failed or disabled Schedule can be destroyed too
  %%> Result:
  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… is marked removing; its trigger and workflow are removed next
  %%> - there is no Node work, so no run starts (R1)
  %%> Not refused:
  %%> - a running fire no longer blocks destroy; `schedule.run_active` is gone
  alt retained Node timer copy
    GW->>SW: Start one run<br/>operation-schedule-destroy
    opt standalone removal
      SW->>N: idle
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - one bounded SSH argv reads the service state
      Note over SW: Check the service is idle
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - native assert accepts inactive, failed or unknown; active and transitional states refuse removal before mutation
    end
    SW->>N: stop_timer
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - official systemd stops and disables the timer
    opt Instance cascade
      SW->>N: stop_service
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - one bounded SSH argv stops the service, including an activating oneshot; standalone removal skips this step
    end
    SW->>N: remove_files
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - official file resources remove four managed paths in parallel
    SW->>N: reload
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one bounded SSH argv reloads systemd after file removal
    SW->>N: check_files
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - official file checks confirm all four paths are absent, in parallel with the timer check
    SW->>N: check_timer
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - official systemd checks the timer is stopped and disabled
    Note over SW: Check the removal
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - native assert requires absent files and a compliant, unloaded timer
    SW-->>GW: Native completion<br/>removal check passed
    Note over GW: Delete the timer copy
    %%> Result:
    %%> - delete only after success; failure keeps the copy recorded as removing
  else a native cron Schedule
    GW->>SW: Remove the trigger<br/>workflow.trigger.remove
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes the cron trigger of `schedule-7f3c…` from serve (Node 209); serve starts no new fire
  %%> - removing a trigger that is gone is safe, so a disabled Schedule takes the same path
  GW->>SW: Delete the workflow<br/>workflow.delete schedule-7f3c…
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - deletes the Schedule's own workflow from serve
  %%> - a fire that already started keeps running from its own copy
  GW->>SW: Delete the command key<br/>vault.delete
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - deletes the Schedule command key after its trigger and workflow
  alt Swamp confirms
    SW-->>GW: trigger and workflow gone
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - serve starts no new fire for Schedule 7f3c…
    Note over GW: Delete the Schedule<br/>its tasks stay as history
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Schedule 7f3c…'s record is deleted
    %%> - its tasks stay, so past fires remain visible
    %%> - task #5141 is recorded as succeeded at once (R1)
    %%> Vault key:
    %%> - the command's vault key is deleted; a fire that already started read it when it began
    GW-->>CLI: Schedule destroyed<br/>202 Accepted · task succeeded
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the result at once; there is nothing to follow
  else Swamp refuses or is down
    SW--xGW: no confirmation<br/>refused or unreachable
    %%> When:
    %%> - serve on 209 does not answer
    Note over GW: Keep Schedule removing<br/>rerun the same destroy
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Schedule 7f3c… stays recorded, marked removing
    %%> - the trigger or the workflow may still be set; a fire it starts still runs and is recorded as a task
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again; removing a trigger or workflow that is gone is safe
    GW--xCLI: 503 removal unconfirmed<br/>swamp.schedule_publish_unavailable
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the error code; the Schedule stays removing
  end
  opt a fire is still running
    SW->>N: Finish the running fire<br/>@swamp/ssh, own timeout
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the fire that already started runs to its end, within the Schedule's timeout
    %%> Why:
    %%> - stopping a command halfway can leave the application in a worse state
    SW-->>GW: Run finished<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the fire<br/>task #5139 · trigger: schedule
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the fire's task, recorded when it started, gets its result, although the Schedule is gone
  end
  end
